From: Opsero <noreply@opsero.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: wilc1000: refuse config requests before the first open

Until the interface is opened for the first time no firmware runs and no
TX thread drains the queue, yet wilc->quit is still 0 (zeroed allocation),
so wilc_wlan_txq_add_cfg_pkt() accepts config packets. A station dump on
the down interface (NL80211_CMD_GET_STATION dump, as systemd-networkd
sends for every Wi-Fi link it enumerates, or `iw dev wlan0 station dump`) reaches
dump_station() -> wilc_get_rssi(), which queues a WID_RSSI query, times
out after 3 s ("Failed to get RSSI value") and leaves the queue entry
behind, together with a pending txq_event completion. The next open
starts the TX thread before it downloads the firmware; the thread wakes
on that completion at once and pushes the stale packet to the chip
(VMM table write, firmware interrupt) while no firmware runs, then keeps
retrying during the download. The chip's TX handshake does not recover:
the firmware never answers the config requests of the start sequence,
each times out ("Failed to configure firmware", "WLAN initialization
FAILED") and the open fails with -EINVAL. The failure path empties the
queue and sets wilc->quit, which is why a second open then works.

 - dump_station(): return -ENOENT (no station) while the firmware is not
   running, as get_station() and get_tx_power() already refuse to talk
   to an uninitialized chip.
 - wilc_cfg80211_init(): start with wilc->quit = 1, the state a close
   leaves behind, so any other config request made before the first
   open is refused instead of being queued.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Opsero <noreply@opsero.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/microchip/wilc1000/cfg80211.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/microchip/wilc1000/cfg80211.c
@@ -1329,6 +1329,10 @@
 	if (idx != 0)
 		return -ENOENT;
 
+	/* No firmware running: no station, and nothing to ask the chip. */
+	if (!vif->wilc->initialized)
+		return -ENOENT;
+
 	ret = wilc_get_rssi(vif, &sinfo->signal);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -1820,6 +1824,15 @@
 
 	wlan_init_locks(wl);
 
+	/*
+	 * The firmware only runs between wilc_wlan_init() (first open) and
+	 * wilc_wlan_cleanup() (close), which clear and set this flag. Start
+	 * in the closed state so a config request that reaches the driver
+	 * before the first open is refused, as it is after a close, instead
+	 * of being left on the TX queue for the next firmware start.
+	 */
+	wl->quit = 1;
+
 	ret = wilc_wlan_cfg_init(wl);
 	if (ret)
 		goto free_wl;
